5. Taking a Heap Dump

For a running JVM:

jps -l 

Example:

12345 com.example.demo.DemoApplication

Then:

jmap -dump:live,format=b,file=heapdump.hprof 12345

Or:

jmap -dump:format=b,file=heapdump.hprof 12345

Difference:

live: requests a dump containing objects considered live after GC.

Without live, more objects may be included.

6. Better production approach — jcmd

Modern JVMs commonly use:

jcmd 12345 GC.heap_dump /tmp/heapdump.hprof

You can first inspect:

jcmd 12345 GC.heap_info

Example:

garbage-first heap   total 2048M, used 1850M
7. Analyze Heap Dump

Popular tools:

Eclipse MAT
VisualVM
JProfiler
YourKit

For example, with Eclipse MAT you might see:

Class                         Shallow Heap    Retained Heap

byte[]                         1 MB           1.2 GB
ArrayList                       24 B           1.2 GB
HashMap                         48 B           500 MB
String                          24 B           300 MB

The important concept is:

Shallow Heap

Memory occupied by the object itself.

Example:

class User {

    String name;
    Address address;
}

The User object itself might consume only a few dozen bytes.

8. Retained Heap

Retained heap is much more important for leak investigation.

Imagine:

CACHE
 |
 +---- User
 |      |
 |      +---- Address
 |      +---- String
 |
 +---- User
        |
        +---- Address

If removing CACHE would make all those objects eligible for GC, then CACHE has a large retained heap.

So when investigating memory leaks:

Look at retained heap, not just shallow heap.



